# Biomedical image review workspace: bounded first release proposal

**Scope.** A local desktop research tool for six researchers who exchange closed project bundles through ordinary shared storage. It supports review and annotation of research images; it is not a diagnostic product. This is an implementable first-stage plan, not a claim that an integrated application or its performance has been tested.

**Decision in brief.** Build a Python/Qt desktop shell around napari’s image and annotation layers, with an application-owned asset/coordinate model, source readers, project persistence, and exporters. Start with local OME-TIFF and OME-Zarr v0.5 plus plain TIFF only when its axes are known or explicitly mapped. Use bounded region reads; never decode the whole source by default. Store annotations and project metadata separately from immutable source bytes. Keep saved coordinates in the source voxel frame with an explicit voxel-to-physical affine. Use a local SQLite project database and immutable content-addressed mask chunks; exchange closed, verified bundles. Treat intensity display as view state. Every resample or geometric transform creates a recorded derived asset.

Version pins below are starting points for an integration spike, not a tested, mutually compatible dependency lock. No complete viewer or application was built for this proposal.

## What the sources establish

**External facts — format and coordinate contract.** OME-NGFF v0.5 describes image axes, names and units; array dimension order is explicit. Its multiscale image transforms use scale and translation in sequence, and its label-image convention nests labels with source information and dimension alignment requirements [S1, §§2.1, 2.3–2.6]. It is a useful interchange contract for common n-dimensional images and integer labels. The cited v0.5 transform model is not a general rotated/sheared affine. The document does not establish the viewer’s voxel-center convention; that convention must be defined by this application.

**External facts — TIFF access.** Tifffile v2026.9.20 documents reading TIFF image data through Zarr-backed arrays and accessing slices/tiles without first materializing the entire series. It covers a subset of TIFF; codec support may require imagecodecs, and the source docs list TIFF variants it cannot decode [S2, release notes; S3, module documentation and examples]. This supports a bounded reader adapter when the file is tiled or segmented appropriately. It does not prove a bound for an arbitrary TIFF: a very large compressed strip or tile still has to be decoded as a unit.

**External facts — chunk indexing failure and repair chain.** Zarr issue #4174 reports a 1.01 TiB `np.bincount` allocation for three coordinate writes on a shape `(10,000,000, 13,854)`, chunked `(1,1)`, with `set_coordinate_selection`; basic and orthogonal selections in the report worked [S4, issue description]. PR #4356 links the issue, was merged on 2026-09-28 as `cd1e5b3aaabf969d5edef18b8d62bf98e3439beb`, and replaces full-grid counts with occupied chunk IDs and run ends for the affected coordinate/integer-array indexers [S5, merge metadata and patch description]. At that merge pin, `sorted_run_ends` explicitly uses memory proportional to the selected items, and the indexer consumes those run ends [S6, `src/zarr/core/indexing.py`, lines 746–760 and 836–838]. Tests at that same merge pin cover sparse selection on a `2**44`-chunk array, round-trip reads/writes, and coordinate/indexer projections on a `2**62`-chunk grid [S7, `tests/test_indexing.py`, lines 1406–1487]. This is an issue → code change → associated regression-test chain, not an inference from an issue title.

The captured latest release was Zarr v3.4.0, published 2026-09-15; the fix merge is dated 2026-09-28 [S8, release metadata; S5]. Therefore the fix is not established as present in v3.4.0. The PR reports 1,195 relevant tests passing, but that is the project’s report; this work did not rerun them. This defect is specific to sparse coordinate/indexing paths and does not establish that ordinary tiled reads or the complete viewer fail. It does establish that “chunked” alone is not a memory guarantee.

**External facts — viewer and lazy scheduling.** Napari 0.5.6 documents Image, Labels, Points and Shapes layers, n-dimensional browsing, visibility/blending, scale/translation and keyboard layer modes. It also warns that 3D rendering of a very large dataset can cause a memory error [S9, “Layers at a glance,” “Layer mode,” “3D rendering of images,” “Scaling layers”]. Its Dask tutorial browses a 3.69 GB unzipped collection of TIFF images and demonstrates on-demand processing. The same page warns that reshaping a lazy array can increase reads dramatically (one example changes a requested slice from one read to 600) [S10, “Side note regarding higher-dimensional datasets,” “A real example,” “Processing data with dask.array.map_blocks”]. This is a useful lazy-viewing and axis-layout precedent, not evidence of a 3 GB single-file test, measured RSS, or latency.

**External facts — project transactions.** SQLite documents all-or-none transactions in rollback-journal mode under assumptions about filesystem locking, flushes and file operations [S11, §§1–3, 9]. Its WAL documentation says WAL does not work over network filesystems and that a live WAL database’s `-wal` state must travel with the database if copied [S12, §§1, 4]. SQLite transactions cover database changes, not a multi-file project bundle or arbitrary shared-storage rename.

## Precedents and why they are independently useful

1. **Tifffile + Zarr, two separate data-access layers.** Tifffile adapts TIFF pages/segments and provides lazy array access; Zarr provides general chunked n-dimensional indexing. The reader/storage adapter and indexing engine have different jobs and can be tested independently. The issue chain above is a consequential warning to keep coordinate writes out of the initial mask path unless the fixed Zarr code is pinned or a released version carries the regression. The PR’s optimization does not remove the need to bound decoded TIFF segment size or cache size.
2. **Napari + Dask, viewer and lazy computation.** Napari supplies inspectable image/label/point/shape layers and axis controls; Dask supplies lazy array scheduling. Their public docs show a workable combination for multi-file microscopy data. They do not supply this proposal’s source identity, project transactions, relocation checks, calibration overrides, or export audit record. The observed read amplification after reshaping is a reason to preserve the reader’s recorded axis mapping and slice plan rather than reshaping blindly.
3. **OME-NGFF, a format-level precedent independent of either viewer.** It supplies explicit axes, units, transforms, multiscales and a label-image structure. Adopt a bounded subset for interchange and keep a richer application coordinate record beside it where the format cannot express the full transform.

## Proposed architecture and version boundary

**Product choice.** Use a Qt desktop shell and an embedded napari canvas for the first UI spike. The application owns project/asset IDs, metadata snapshots, axis mapping, calibration, source-to-derived lineage, coordinate transforms, annotations, history, and export manifests. Napari layers are a view/editor surface; their in-memory state is not the durable project format. Keep 2D slice viewing as the reliable default. Offer a bounded 3D preview only after a separate memory gate because napari’s own documentation warns about large 3D inputs.

**Reader boundary.** Implement a `SourceReader` interface that returns: source metadata bytes when available; parsed metadata plus parser/version; raw dimension names/order, sizes and sample dtype; candidate calibration; supported status and reason; and cancellable region reads. The first adapters are:

- Tifffile `v2026.9.20` for supported local OME-TIFF and plain TIFF. Read a selected series via tile/page slices or a Zarr-backed view; do not call an eager whole-image conversion for display.
- Zarr Python `v3.4.0` for a tested local OME-Zarr v0.5 subset. Read a chosen resolution level and chunk-aligned regions. Avoid `set_coordinate_selection` for routine mask edits at this pin. If the app needs that path, either wait for a maintained release containing the regression tests or evaluate the exact merge commit in a separately locked prototype; do not silently rely on a git branch in a user project.
- Dask may schedule lazy reads, but preserve dimensions and map source axes explicitly. Any reshape/rechunk/proxy operation is explicit and included in validation.

**Proposed spike pins:** napari `0.5.6`, tifffile `2026.9.20`, Zarr Python `3.4.0`, Python `3.12`. Pin Qt bindings, Dask, imagecodecs and transitive dependencies only after a clean-machine compatibility build. These pins identify the evidence surfaces, not a compatibility result. A candidate update must rerun source-format fixtures and the Zarr sparse-index regression before adoption.

A plausible alternative is a small custom Qt/VisPy slice canvas. It offers direct control over coordinate handling and a smaller viewer API surface, but the team would own image-layer blending, shape/point interaction, navigation and rendering accessibility. Keep this as a fallback if napari’s layer coordinate or memory behavior cannot meet the acceptance checks; do not implement both in the first slice.

## Minimum workflow and support boundary

1. **Create project and add source.** Create a local project directory; reference the original file/tree read-only. Write an asset row in `IMPORTING` state before long work. Record path, size/stat snapshot and raw metadata. Hash bytes in a cancelable streaming job. For a Zarr directory, hash a canonical sorted manifest of relative key, byte length and file digest. Mark identity `PENDING` until complete; do not bind exports to an unverified digest. Detect source mutation during file hashing and restart or report it.
2. **Parse and inspect.** Show file/container, selected series/level, original axis strings/order, sizes, dtype, calibration and provenance, parser version, warnings, identity status and source path. Preserve original metadata bytes (or a lossless metadata capture) separately from parsed/overridden values. Unknown axis or calibration is visible. Offer explicit axis-role mapping, unit/origin/spacing/orientation correction, series selection, or metadata-only import. Save each override with author/time/reason; never overwrite source metadata.
3. **Navigate and display.** Show selected channels, z slice, time point, sample dtype, physical spacing and original/derived badge. Window/level, gamma/LUT, visibility and blending are view state. They operate on display buffers only. Never write these mapped values back to source samples. Maintain separate original and derived layers with textual badges and lineage.
4. **Annotate.** Provide points, polygons and integer masks; keyboard navigation across image, channel, z and time; reusable named/versioned styles; and transactional undo/redo. Show the annotation scope (plane, volume or whole acquisition) in text. Save author/time and target asset identity.
5. **Save and close.** Commit edits through a local project transaction. At close, verify pending operations are finished/canceled and the project is consistent. Do not support concurrent edits to one shared database in v1.
6. **Exchange and reopen.** Export a closed portable bundle to shared storage, then reopen on another machine. In reference mode, prompt to relocate missing paths and bind only when the content digest matches. On mismatch, keep annotations attached to the old asset and ask the user to import the new bytes as a new asset or explicitly create a documented replacement mapping.
7. **Export and verify.** Produce annotation table/geometry, a transparent overlay for a specified plane/time, and a small derived crop. Show source identity, coordinate frame, selected axes, crop bounds, dtype, transformations, output types and known losses in a manifest. Compare source identity before/after and inspect output sample values and coordinate mapping.

**Support boundary (product choice).** Test and support local, readable filesystems only. First fixtures: (a) OME-TIFF with one selected logical series and supported TIFF segment codecs; (b) local OME-Zarr v0.5 with 2–5 dimensions, interpretable axes and a chosen scale level; (c) plain TIFF only if axes/calibration are unambiguous or the user maps them. Start with `uint8`, `uint16`, `int16`, `uint32`, `int32`, `float32` and `float64`; reject complex, packed or other dtypes until a fixture justifies them. Keep all declared singleton dimensions rather than silently squeezing them. Support one series/level at a time; no stitching, remote object stores, proprietary instrument readers, arbitrary TIFF codec promise, or whole-volume rendering guarantee. The exact v0.5 OME-Zarr subset and codec matrix are fixture-defined, not inferred from the format name.

For a recognized but unsupported asset, retain its reference, captured original metadata and failure reason in the project as `UNSUPPORTED`/metadata-only. Do not produce image annotations until axes and coordinate interpretation are explicit. A parse/decode failure must leave the project openable and source unchanged. User can choose a supported series/axis map, create a clearly marked local proxy, or remove the failed asset record. No import path rewrites an original.

## Coordinate and annotation data contract

**Product choice — explicit convention.** Preserve raw array axes exactly as read. Store a mapping from each raw axis index to its declared role (`T`, `C`, `Z`, `Y`, `X`, or named unknown), with units and size. Never infer an unknown axis as channel/time from its length. The visible spatial coordinate order is `(x,y,z)`; time/channel are selectors, not implicit spatial dimensions.

Define an application coordinate as a continuous, zero-based **voxel-center** coordinate: integer `(x,y,z)` locates the center of that source sample. Store a double-precision homogeneous `H_source_to_physical` affine mapping this source voxel frame into declared physical units; store the raw-axis permutation/direction that maps array indices to `(x,y,z)`, source origin, units, coordinate convention, and the unmodified source transform metadata. If physical calibration is absent, label coordinates in `px` and leave physical calibration unknown; never invent micrometers. The viewport transform from image coordinates to screen coordinates is transient view state and never becomes annotation geometry.

OME-NGFF v0.5’s scale/translation transforms are useful for the supported straight-axis case, but its documented model is not enough for arbitrary orientation/shear [S1, §2.3]. Keep the exact app affine in project and export sidecar. Emit conforming NGFF transform metadata only when the transform can be represented without loss; otherwise label the NGFF representation as incomplete and let the sidecar remain authoritative. Reject singular or contradictory calibration unless the user explicitly supplies a correction.

A crop with offset vector `c` has `H_child = H_parent * T(c)`, where `T(c)` maps child voxel coordinates into parent voxel coordinates. A resample records the full output-to-parent coordinate map, interpolation kernel, fill value, output shape, dtype and software/version. New annotations drawn on a derived view are mapped back to the parent only when that map is valid and invertible; otherwise they are explicitly anchored to the derived asset. Do not silently move geometry after an operation.

**Small executed witness.** Candidate-authored Python used origin `(10,-3) µm`, scale `(.25,.5) µm/pixel`, source point `(9,7)`, and crop offset `(4,3)`. Before and after rebasing, the physical location was `(12.25,0.5) µm`; the cropped point was `(5,4)` and crop origin `(11,-1.5) µm`. It exited 0 with no stderr. This checks the proposed translation arithmetic only; it does not test OME parsing, a reader, a GUI, resampling, or application behavior. Full receipt is in `witnesses.json`.

Each annotation has an immutable annotation ID; target asset ID and source digest; geometry type; coordinates in its declared asset voxel frame; axis scope; category; style ID/version; author and timestamps; and edit-history reference. A point or polygon can be restricted to a selected `(t,z)` plane, span z at one time point (volume), or span acquisition time; record fixed axes and span axes explicitly. Channel applicability is `all` or an explicit channel set. For a mask, store integer label chunks with their coordinate frame and chunk geometry; edit only affected chunks. Do not store screen coordinates.

Store points/polygons and edits in SQLite. Store each changed mask chunk as an immutable content-addressed blob, then transactionally update SQLite’s chunk-to-blob pointer and undo history. A crash before the pointer transaction can leave an unreferenced blob, which recovery can garbage-collect; a committed pointer must never refer to a mutable in-place chunk. Persist reusable style definitions/versions and keyboard-binding preferences in the project or user profile with a clear project-vs-user scope.

## Project storage, identity, recovery and relocation

**Product choice.** One local project directory contains a versioned SQLite database, immutable mask blobs, a manifest and optional derived files. Use one application writer and SQLite rollback-journal mode for the first release; avoid a live project database on shared storage. The six researchers exchange closed bundles rather than editing a common database. This keeps the transaction boundary within one local database; it does not promise conflict-free collaboration.

Commit normal metadata/annotation edits in a SQLite transaction. Before a mask-pointer transaction, write the new blob under a unique digest name, verify it, and flush it; then atomically commit its pointer and edit record. Recovery opens the last committed database, runs an integrity check, verifies referenced blobs as needed, and reports missing/corrupt blobs without discarding other annotations. Orphan blobs are cleaned only after verification. Import metadata is staged and marks `IMPORTING`/`FAILED`/`READY`; a partial import never masquerades as a completed asset. Store operation progress and cancellation status. On interrupted export, leave the previous ready bundle intact and remove or expose the incomplete staging output for recovery.

File path is a locator, not identity. Use an asset UUID plus SHA-256 of the source file; for directory stores use a deterministic manifest digest of all relative keys and bytes. Hash streaming is cancelable and may take time for multi-gigabyte sources. On reopen, a missing path can be relocated; match content before reconnecting. Same path with different bytes is a mismatch. Preserve original metadata and every user correction; keep original and derived assets as separate IDs.

**Portability choices.** `Reference bundle` stores relative/external asset locators, source digests, metadata and annotations; it is fast/small but images may be offline until relinked. `Portable bundle` copies original bytes unchanged and includes project state; it is self-contained but can multiply storage and take time for 3 GB sources. Offer both with explicit labels and free-space estimates. Derived proxy data is always marked derived and never presented as original. Bundle only after closing/checkpointing cleanly; keep SQLite and any journal state together if the app ever adds WAL. SQLite’s documented guarantees depend on filesystem/OS behavior [S11]; WAL is explicitly unsuitable for network filesystems [S12].

Build each exchange bundle in a unique temporary sibling, include a manifest and hashes, validate the database and all copied members, then publish to a new versioned name. Use replacement/rename only after validating semantics on the target shared-storage type. If rename behavior is uncertain, write a versioned directory and a small ready marker last; never overwrite the last known-good bundle. This directory-level protocol is an application choice, not a guarantee furnished by SQLite.

## Display, transforms and honest export

- **View-only display:** save channel visibility, window/level, gamma/LUT, slice/time, pan/zoom and layer visibility/blending as view state. These controls do not alter source samples.
- **Derived data operation:** crop, orientation change, resampling, intensity conversion, rechunking/proxy creation, or other data-writing action gets a new derived asset ID and parent ID+digest, operation ID/type, exact transform, interpolation/fill, input/output axes/shape/dtype, software/version, time/actor and parameters. Source remains read-only. A lossless rechunk is still a recorded derived representation.
- **Annotation export:** write a canonical versioned JSONL table with one object per annotation, plus a convenience CSV with stable columns and JSON geometry/scope fields. Include asset ID/digest, coordinate convention, affine, units, axis scope, channel scope, style, mask references and export manifest. CSV alone is not a lossless mask format.
- **Overlay:** generate a transparent RGBA PNG for a chosen z/time/channel view. Manifest records plane, affine, palette and rasterization settings. PNG is a rendered overlay and loses editable vector structure if used alone.
- **Small derived image:** export a selected crop via bounded source reads. Default operation is a sample-preserving crop retaining original dtype and values, without applying window/level or resampling. Record parent digest, original voxel bounding box, axes and child affine. Include calibration in OME-TIFF when faithfully expressible and always include the precise app sidecar; report when other readers may ignore it. Hash source before/after to verify no in-place change.
- **Mask interchange:** canonical JSONL plus mask data is authoritative. Emit an OME-NGFF v0.5 label group only when axes, shape and multiscale relationships satisfy that spec’s label requirements [S1, §2.6]. Otherwise export a clearly described companion mask asset; do not claim the label group preserves unsupported metadata.

Export UI must display what was copied, transformed, rendered or omitted. Never call a PNG overlay the annotation source of truth, or describe display contrast mapping as preserved sample values.

## Responsiveness and accessibility targets

**Engineering inference.** A multi-gigabyte image can be browsed on a 16 GB workstation if source access, decoded blocks, cache and viewport buffers are all bounded. The tifffile/Dask precedents show useful mechanisms, not proof that this target is already met. Large single-segment TIFFs and huge Zarr chunks can violate the bound even when the application is lazy.

**Proposed product targets (UNEXECUTED).** Start with a 512 MiB decoded-block LRU; cap a single decoded segment/chunk at 64 MiB; allow at most two decode jobs in flight (visible request plus one prefetch); keep display buffers bounded and evictable. This budgets roughly 512 MiB cache + 128 MiB in-flight + about 128 MiB of viewport buffers before library overhead. Measure actual process RSS; acceptance target is no more than 1 GiB RSS growth above idle while navigating the 3 GB fixture. Cap workers at two initially. Emit progress for hash/import/proxy/export, expose a cancel action, and check cancellation between chunks/segments. A codec may not be interruptible mid-segment; the UI must state that cancellation will take effect after the active decode. Proposed cancel response target is two seconds after the active segment returns. Benchmark cold and warm slice changes and report p50/p95 rather than promising a universal latency.

If a source segment/chunk exceeds 64 MiB, explain the measured size before reading it. Offer an explicit local chunked proxy/rechunk operation with required scratch-space estimate, progress and cancellation, or leave the source metadata-only/unsupported. Proxy generation is derived and source-preserving. Never silently allocate a full decoded copy to “make it work.”

Keyboard operation must cover project open/save, axes/channel/slice/time navigation, annotation tools, undo/redo, style selection, export and dialogs. Show shortcut hints and allow remapping. Use text and icon labels for original/derived and errors, not color alone. Keep metadata and error text legible, focus errors for keyboard/screen readers, and give actionable messages with file, axis and recovery option; a traceback must not be the only feedback.

## Validation plan before a useful release (all UNEXECUTED)

1. **Format fixtures:** successful and failing OME-TIFF, plain TIFF and OME-Zarr fixtures with multiple axis permutations, singleton dimensions, integer/float samples, known calibration, missing/contradictory calibration, multiple series, unsupported codecs and oversized segments/chunks. Compare parsed metadata with known fixtures and confirm unknown dimensions remain visible.
2. **Bounded access:** one tiled/segmented 3 GB TIFF and one equivalent chunked source. Measure process RSS, decoded chunk/segment maximum, disk I/O, slice-update p50/p95 and cancellation at each stage on the target 8-core/16 GB class machine. Repeat cold and warm. Confirm no full-sized second allocation. This is the discriminating check for the brief’s engineering target.
3. **Coordinate contract:** imported known points across axis permutations and unit changes; verify pixel-center physical coordinates, crop translation, origin/direction and export round trip. Test rotated/reflected orientations and reject unsupported/singular transforms. The executed witness above covers arithmetic only.
4. **Display/operation separation:** save varied window/level/LUT and prove original file digest and sample values are unchanged. For crop/resample/proxy, compare output values, dtype and transforms to declared operation; check parent/child IDs and derived badge.
5. **Identity/relocation:** close, move project and asset, reopen, relink a byte-identical file and reject a same-path changed file. For directory stores test renamed files, missing chunks and changed metadata/chunk bytes against the manifest digest.
6. **Crash recovery:** inject termination during metadata import, mask chunk write, SQLite transaction, bundle copy, verification and final publish. Reopen and check committed state, blob reachability, checksums and previous ready bundle. Test the selected ordinary shared-storage filesystems for locks, rename and ready-marker behavior.
7. **Export round trip:** verify JSONL/CSV fields, mask raster, chosen overlay plane, crop bounding box and pixel values, dtype, calibration sidecar and manifest. Confirm the source hash stays identical and independently inspect the export with a supported reader. Explicitly test which metadata another reader ignores.
8. **Sparse indexing regression:** if any annotation path uses Zarr coordinate writes, run the large sparse-selection regressions at S7 against the selected release and add an application-level memory test. Until the fix reaches a maintained release, either pin/review S6’s exact commit for a prototype or use chunk/block writes that avoid the failing path.
9. **Usability/accessibility:** run keyboard-only create/navigate/annotate/undo/save/reopen/export tasks, dialog focus checks, screen-reader labels, readable metadata/error messages and original/derived distinction with color removed.

## Optional opportunities and deferred alternatives

- **Useful opportunity:** conforming OME-Zarr v0.5 image and label interchange can make a future handoff less application-specific. Keep the full source affine and other application metadata in a sidecar when the standard representation is insufficient. Multi-scale export, plate/well collections and broader instrument parsing require separate fixture work.
- **Alternative storage tradeoff:** reference bundles keep six users from repeatedly copying very large originals but depend on stable storage and relocation checks. Portable bundles are safer to carry offline and easier to archive, at the cost of bytes and wait time. Support both explicitly.
- **Deferred:** multi-user concurrent edits, cloud sync, auto-segmentation, registration, GPU acceleration and remote-object readers are not minimum obligations. Revisit only with a defined conflict/provenance model, representative data and measurable benefit. A Bio-Formats bridge or format plugin is a later expansion path after the core reader interface and unsupported-input behavior are stable; it must not become a universal-compatibility promise.

## Principal unresolved dependencies

The first spike must settle (a) napari/Qt/Dask/tifffile/Zarr version compatibility and custom axis transform support, (b) the per-codec TIFF decoded-segment size and cancel behavior, (c) whether the selected image formats encode every required orientation/unit unambiguously, and (d) whether the group’s shared-storage systems provide reliable close/rename/locking semantics for bundles. Keep the support matrix narrow until the proposed checks establish those points. The only executed check here is the isolated coordinate arithmetic witness; all application, memory, crash, export and accessibility checks remain proposed/UNEXECUTED.